448 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Every behavior your dog learns exists within a context. And that context is shaped by three variables that we call the 3 D's: Duration, Distance, and Distractions.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
449 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Understanding these three factors is one of the most important things you can do as a trainer. They explain why your dog can perform perfectly at home but struggles at the park. Why a behavior that lasted ten seconds yesterday falls apart after three seconds today.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
450 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (The 3 D's are not obstacles. They are tools. When you learn to manage them, you can set your dog up for success in any situation.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
451 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (The key principle is simple: if one D increases, another should decrease. This keeps the challenge manageable and your dog confident.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
452 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (25)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The Three Variables)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
454 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Duration)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
455 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (How long your dog maintains a behavior. This could be holding a sit, maintaining eye contact, or staying in position during a routine.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
456 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Duration is built gradually. Start with one second. Then two. Then five. Never jump ahead faster than your dog can handle.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (If your dog breaks the behavior, you have pushed too far. Go back to a duration they can succeed at and build again.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Distance)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The physical space between you and your dog, or between your dog and a distraction. Distance changes everything.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (A dog who can sit perfectly beside you may struggle when you step three feet away. A dog who ignores a treat on the floor may lose focus when the treat is right in front of their nose.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Distance is a variable you control. Use it wisely.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
462 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Distractions)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
463 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Everything in the environment that competes for your dog's attention. Other dogs, sounds, smells, people, movement.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
464 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Distractions are the hardest variable to manage because you cannot always control them. But you can control your response to them.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
465 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Start training in low-distraction environments. Build success there first. Then gradually introduce more challenge.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
